1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count_Franz_Moritz_von_Lacy
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.exulanten.com/wildgeese.html '''wildgeese'''...] ...; [http://www.geneall.net/U/per_page.php?id=1393210 geneall.net...] ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Who was the Irishman who rescued the Emperor? Maximilian Karl Lamoral Graf O’Donnell von Tyrconnell (1812-1895) was born in Vienna, a descendant of the Irish noble dynasty of O'Donnell of Tyrconnell and son of Count Maurice O'Donn
2 CONC ell (Moritz Graf O’Donnell) (1780-1843). He was educated in Dresden, then joined the military and served in several engagements in Europe, including in Italy in 1848, and Hungary in 1849, resulting in many awards and promotion. He
2 CONC  became aide-de- camp to Emperor Franz Josef I.  
2 CONT
2 CONT O’Donnell held a German Habsburg title of Count, granted to his great-grandfather, and after rescuing the Emperor, he was made a Count of the Habsburg Austrian Empire. Maximilian was also honoured with orders of chivalry by severa
2 CONC l European monarchs, and made a Freeman of the cities of Vienna, Prague, Pest, Laibach and others. He was also conferred with the Commander's Cross of the Royal Order of Leopold. He died in his home in Salzburg, and is buried in t
2 CONC he Salzburg Cemetery. But why was he there in the first place?
2 CONT
2 CONT Several Irishmen were famous in the Habsburg Empire. Much earlier, in 1634, during the Thirty Years' War, Irish officers led by Walter Deveraux assassinated general Albrecht von Wallenstein on the orders of the Habsburg Emperor. T
2 CONC hen there was the "Flight of the Wild Geese", which technically refers to the departure of an Irish Jacobite army under the command of Patrick Sarsfield from Ireland to France after the end of the Williamite War in Ireland. But mo
2 CONC re broadly, the term "Wild Geese" refers to Irish soldiers who left to serve in continental European armies in the 16th, 17th and 18th centuries, or even to Irish soldiers in British armies as late as the First World War. As well 
2 CONC as going into service for the governments of France, Spain and elsewhere, a substantial numbers of Irish officers and men were in the armies of the Austrian Habsburg Empire, many of whom were based in Prague. The most famous of th
2 CONC ese was Peter Lacy, Russian Field Marshal, whose son Franz Moritz von Lacy excelled in the Austrian service. Also well known is Lacy's colleague and commanding officer, General Count Maximilian Ulysses von Browne, the Austrian Com
2 CONC mander-in-Chief at the Battle of Lobositz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Austrian field marshal Franz Moritz Graf von Lacy or Lascy 1725 – 1801, was born at St Petersburg to Count Peter Lacy, a Russian Field Marshal who belonged to an Irish family that had followed the exiled James II.  Franz Moritz wa
2 CONC s educated in Germany for a military career before entering the Austrian service. He served in Italy, Bohemia, Silesia and the Netherlands during the War of the Austrian Succession, was twice wounded, and by the end of the war was
2 CONC  a lieutenant-colonel. He became full colonel and chief of an infantry regiment at age twenty-five. With the advent of the Seven Years' War in 1756, he distinguished himself in the first battle (Lobositz) and was promoted major-ge
2 CONC neral, after receiving his third wound. His fourth wound was at the battle of Prague in 1757. Later that year, he took part in the celebrated victory of Breslau. He was wounded for the fifth time at Leuthen, but covered the retrea
2 CONC t of the defeated army. He and others made things difficult for Friedrich the Great for the remainder of the war. Lacy was made quartermaster-general, After Hochkirch on October 15, 1758, Lacy received the Grand Cross of the Order
2 CONC  of Maria Theresa. Later, after Maria Theresa placed her son, Emperor Joseph II, at the head of Austrian military affairs, Lacy was made a field marshal, and given the task of reforming and administering the army (1766). He framed
2 CONC  new regulations for each arm and a new code of military law. Due to ill health, in 1773, he went to southern France. When Joseph II  became Austrian emperor upon Maria Theresa's death, Lacy remained his most trusted friend. He to
2 CONC ok part in several more military actions and retired to his castle of Neuwaldegg near Vienna. Wild Geese and Irishmen
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 18, 1853, Emperor Franz Joseph survived an assassination attempt. He was walking with an officer when a Hungarian nationalist named János Libényi sprang out and stabbed him in the neck. His stiff collar deflected the k
2 CONC nife. Officer Maximilian Karl Lamoral Graf O’Donnell von Tyrconnell immediately struck Libényi down with his sabre, an act for which he was rewarded and made a Count of the Habsburg Empire. Another witness who helped Franz was a b
2 CONC utcher named Joseph Ettenreich who was later elevated to nobility by the Emperor and became Joseph von Ettenreich. Libényi was tried, condemned to death for attempted regicide and executed on the Simmeringer Haide. After the unsuc
2 CONC cessful attack the Emperor's brother Ferdinand Maximilian Joseph called upon the Europe's Royal families for donations to a new church on the site of the attack. The church was to be a Votivgabe (a thank-you to God) for the rescue
2 CONC  of the Emperor and is called the Votivkirche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry
